# 🖥️ GDEW0154Z17 E-Paper Display

## 📖 Overview
This project demonstrates how to use the **GDEW0154Z17 1.54-inch three-color E-Paper Display** with a microcontroller (e.g., Arduino, ESP32, or STM32). The display communicates through the **SPI interface** and can show graphics, text, and images in **black, white, and red**. It offers ultra-low power consumption and retains the displayed image even when power is removed. :contentReference[oaicite:0]{index=0}

## ✨ Features
- 1.54-inch three-color E-Paper display
- Resolution: **152 × 152 pixels**
- Displays text, graphics, and images
- SPI communication interface
- Ultra-low power consumption
- Sunlight readable with a wide viewing angle
- Image remains visible without power (bistable display) :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}

## 🛠️ Hardware Required
- Arduino Uno / ESP32 / STM32 (or compatible board)
- GDEW0154Z17 E-Paper Display
- Jumper wires
- Breadboard (optional)

## 🔌 Typical Wiring (SPI)

| Display Pin | Arduino Uno |
|-------------|-------------|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| DIN (MOSI) | D11 |
| CLK (SCK) | D13 |
| CS | D10 |
| DC | D9 |
| RST | D8 |
| BUSY | D7 |

> **Note:** The display operates at **3.3 V logic**. Ensure your hardware supports the required voltage levels. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}

## 📚 Libraries Used
- GxEPD2
- Adafruit GFX

## ▶️ How It Works
1. The microcontroller initializes the E-Paper display.
2. Text or graphics are written into the display buffer.
3. The display performs a full refresh to show the new content.
4. The image remains visible even after power is removed until the next refresh.

